  <h2>Read the story</h2>

  <h3>The fire</h3>
  <p>
    Ryu's hand closed on the red handle, and the blade remembered him.
  </p>
  <p>
    It did not come slowly. Fire ran along the steel like water
    finding a crack — red, hungry, alive — and the night of the
    burning village turned to day around him. Three years he had
    buried it. Three years of rice fields and quiet mornings. In one
    breath, all of it was gone.
  </p>
  <p>
    The soldiers stepped back. Even Kaito's men, who had marched in
    perfect order, knew what they were looking at. This was not a
    torch. This was the Fire Sword.
  </p>

  <h3>Kaito smiles</h3>
  <p>
    Kaito did not step back. He stood in the firelight, black armor
    drinking the glow, and smiled the way a man smiles when a long
    wait is finally over.
  </p>
  <p>
    “Finally,” he said. “The fire remembers you.”
  </p>
  <p>
    Ryu understood, in that moment, that this had been the plan all
    along. Kaito had not come to Kaze for gold or rice. He had come
    to make Ryu pick up the sword. The village was the bait. The fire
    was the catch.
  </p>

  <h3>Aiko</h3>
  <p>
    “Ryu!” Aiko's voice cut through the roar of the flames. A
    soldier held her at the edge of the fire, where the heat made the
    air shimmer, his blade at her shoulder. She was frightened — but
    she was not begging. She was looking at Ryu the way she had
    looked at him in the temple: don't do this. Don't pay the price.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ryu did it anyway. He always would. That was the price, and the
    price was hers to pay too, if he let her burn.
  </p>

  <h3>Fire against men</h3>
  <p>
    He moved like the fire itself. One burning arc, and the first
    soldier's spear was ash. Another, and the men who had held Aiko
    were running, their shields smoking. The blade did not feel
    heavy. It felt like coming home — and that was the most
    frightening thing of all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then his hand began to burn. Not the sword. His hand. The fire
    was eating the skin of his palm, thin as paper, and Master
    Hayato's voice came back to him from the training yard, years
    ago: every use burns a part of the man who holds it.
  </p>

  <h3>Ice</h3>
  <p>
    Kaito drew his sword at last. Ice-blue steel, cold mist rolling
    off the edge, frost blooming across the burning street wherever
    the blade pointed. Fire on one side of the square. Ice on the
    other. The village held its breath between them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Aiko broke free in the confusion and ran — straight into Ryu's
    arms, her face buried against his torn kimono. He lowered the
    burning blade, just a little, just enough to hold her.
  </p>
  <p>
    Kaito watched them, and his smile did not fade. He had what he
    came for. The fire was awake. And a fire that is awake can be
    seen — from very, very far away.
  </p>
